UID:mondriaan-publication-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Publication
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Publication grant supports the production 
 of special publications about living visual artists from the Netherlands o
 r the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. It funds books and other publications
  that give substantial\, high-quality attention to an artist's work and pr
 actice. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists and publishers\, individually or joi
 ntly\; the featured artist(s) must be professional and have been active fo
 r at least one year\, based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of th
 e Kingdom. AMOUNT: the Mondriaan Fund contributes a maximum of 35% of the 
 eligible expenses\; the available budget for this round is EUR 205\,000. A
 PPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online application portal\
 , which requires a pre-created account (allow several working days to set 
 up). DEADLINE: 30 July 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (10:00 Caribbean time).\n\n
 Organisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Netherlands. Fea
 tured artist(s) must be professional visual artists based in the Netherlan
 ds or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishers may co-apply.\nAward:
  Contribution of up to 35% of eligible expenses. Total budget for this rou
 nd is EUR 205\,000.\nDuration: Project-based (production of a publication)
 .\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publi
 cation-2/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publication-2
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Featured artist(s) must be professional visual artist
 s based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishe
 rs may co-apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260827
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Residency grant supports professional visu
 al artists\, curators\, and art observers who want to deepen their practic
 e through a residency in the Netherlands or abroad. It contributes to the 
 costs of a self-arranged residency that offers meaningful development for 
 the applicant's work. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists\, curators\, and art o
 bservers who have been professionally active for at least one year and are
  based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. AMOUNT: th
 e grant amount varies depending on the residency's location and duration\;
  applicants must contribute at least 10% of the costs themselves\, through
  personal investment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\,
  or crowdfunding. APPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online 
 application portal\, which requires a pre-created account (allow several w
 orking days). DEADLINE: 27 August 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (11:00 Caribbean
  time).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Nethe
 rlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part 
 of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherlands or abroad.\
 nAward: Grant amount varies depending on the location and duration of the 
 residency. Applicants must contribute at least 10% themselves (personal in
 vestment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\, or crowdfun
 ding).\nDuration: Varies by residency.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds
 .nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Ca
 ribbean part of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherland
 s or abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:prospect-art-new-work-we-come-in-pieces-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: Prospect Art: NEW WORK Commission 2027 - "We Come in Piec
 es"
DESCRIPTION:Prospect Art invites visual artists worldwide to apply for the 
 2027 NEW WORK commission\, a USD 1\,500 project grant supporting the creat
 ion of a new\, conceptually driven work responding to the curatorial theme
  "We Come in Pieces\," developed by 2026-2027 Curatorial Fellow Alma Samme
 l. Through a single application\, artists are also considered for ONE WORK
  (an online series featuring an in-depth conversation centered on a single
  completed artwork) and BROADCAST (a curated online program of screenings\
 , artist talks\, and discussions). THEME: "We Come in Pieces" proposes the
  Alien as a method for shifting perspective\, approaching alienness as a p
 ractice of estrangement to encounter the world beyond familiar human-cente
 red narratives. It explores relationships between human and more-than-huma
 n worlds\, speculative cosmologies\, ecological systems\, technologies\, l
 andscapes\, histories\, infrastructures\, and nonhuman forms of intelligen
 ce (satellites and orbital debris\, fungal networks\, oceans\, atmospheric
  systems\, algorithmic intelligences)\, inviting works that displace the p
 oint of listening\, seeing\, sensing\, or knowing. Research-based and conc
 eptually driven practices are especially encouraged. WHAT'S OFFERED: NEW W
 ORK\, USD 1\,500 to one artist plus a featured website section and social 
 promotion (project completed within 12 months)\; ONE WORK\, a published ar
 ticle and social promotion\; BROADCAST\, a USD 125 screening or speaking f
 ee and inclusion in a themed program. ELIGIBILITY: professional visual art
 ists working in any medium at any career stage\, except those currently en
 rolled in undergraduate or graduate studio art programs\; collectives may 
 apply. Media welcomed include moving image\, installation\, sculpture\, ph
 otography\, performance\, sound\, digital media\, social practice\, and in
 terdisciplinary forms. APPLICATION: submit via the official Google Form wi
 th contact information\, a project description (250 words)\, a sketch/visu
 alization (1-3 images)\, up to 3 links to completed work samples\, a state
 ment relating the project to the theme (200 words)\, a short bio and exhib
 ition history (500 words)\, and a budget breakdown of the USD 1\,500. A US
 D 10 administrative fee is paid via PayPal (include PayPal name and transa
 ction ID)\; a fee waiver is available with no explanation by emailing subm
 issions@prospectart.org before the deadline. Writers may email gioj@prospe
 ctart.org with samples. DEADLINE: 16 August 2026\; results for the NEW WOR
 K grant notified by end of September. INFO SESSION: 5 August 2026 at noon 
 PST.\n\nOrganisation: Prospect Art\nLocation: Remote / online. Open to vis
 ual artists worldwide\; programs (ONE WORK\, BROADCAST) run online.\nAward
 : NEW WORK: USD 1\,500 commission for one artist\, plus a featured website
  section and social promotion. BROADCAST: USD 125 screening or speaking fe
 e if selected. ONE WORK: a published article (exposure). NOTE: a USD 10 ad
 ministrative fee applies to apply\, with a no-questions-asked waiver avail
 able on request before the deadline. PayPal account required to receive fu
 nds.\nDuration: The commissioned NEW WORK project should be completed with
 in twelve months of selection.\n\nMore: https://www.prospectart.org/new-wo
 rk-grant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.prospectart.org/new-work-grant?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / online. Open to visual artists worldwide\; programs (ONE 
 WORK\, BROADCAST) run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:black-arts-centre-call-for-submissions-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
SUMMARY:Deadline: Black Arts Centre: Call for Exhibition and Programming Pr
 oposals
DESCRIPTION:The Black Arts Centre is calling for proposals from artists for
  exhibitions and public programming. Exhibition proposals are considered f
 or Spring 2027 and beyond\, while programming proposals are considered for
  projects taking place from September 2026 onward. The submission portal i
 s open from 1 June to 8 August 2026. Proposals are reviewed by the Black A
 rts Centre team and a revolving committee of external community members\, 
 evaluated using an internal rubric\; applicants begin to hear back in Sept
 ember. SUPPORT: successful proposals receive financial\, budgeting\, and c
 uratorial support from the Black Arts Centre\, including project execution
 \, production\, logistics\, and marketing. ELIGIBILITY AND PRIORITY: appli
 cants do not need to identify as Black to submit\, but priority is given t
 o Black artists\, curators\, and art organizers\, and to artists and proje
 cts that align with the gallery's mandate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, Immi
 grant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives). Submission does not guarantee approv
 al for this cycle\, but all proposals are kept on file for future consider
 ation\, and updated or alternate proposals are accepted. Questions and PDF
  proposals/portfolios: info@theblackartscentre.ca. Deadline 8 August 2026.
 \n\nOrganisation: Black Arts Centre\nLocation: Surrey\, British Columbia\,
  Canada. Priority to Black artists\, curators\, and art organizers\, and t
 o projects aligned with the gallery's mandate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, 
 Immigrant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives).\nAward: Successful proposals rec
 eive financial\, budgeting\, and curatorial support from the Black Arts Ce
 ntre\, including project execution\, production\, logistics\, and marketin
 g. No submission fee.\nDuration: Exhibition proposals considered for Sprin
 g 2027 and beyond\; programming proposals for projects taking place from S
 eptember 2026 onward. Submission portal open 1 June to 8 August 2026\; app
 licants begin to hear back in September.\n\nMore: https://theblackartscent
 re.ca/submissions/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://theblackartscentre.ca/submissions/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Surrey\, British Columbia\, Canada. Priority to Black artists\, cu
 rators\, and art organizers\, and to projects aligned with the gallery's m
 andate (BIPOC\, Disabled\, Queer\, Immigrant/Refugee/Newcomer perspectives
 ).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gallery-penn-college-exhibitions-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Gallery at Penn College: 2027-28 Call for Exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:The Gallery at Penn College (Pennsylvania College of Technology
 \, Williamsport\, PA) invites artists to apply for solo\, two-person\, or 
 group exhibitions for its 2027-28 season. All entries are juried through a
  blind selection process by a gallery committee of faculty\, staff\, K-12 
 art educators\, and community artists\; review takes place during the Fall
  semester and artists receive notification in January. PROVIDED: housing\,
  meals\, a solo exhibition honorarium\, and a shipping and travel stipend.
  Promotional support includes an annual gallery catalog\, postcards\, pres
 s releases\, and web and social media. THE SPACE: the Gallery is 3\,000 sq
 uare feet with 82-250 running feet of linear wall space and an 11'4" ceili
 ng. All exhibitions are installed by Gallery staff\; site-specific install
 ations requiring artist installation need a planning document approved bef
 ore scheduling. Artists must have enough work for a solo exhibition unless
  applying for a two-person show. ENGAGEMENT: campus visits are an importan
 t part of programming (some exhibitions are scheduled without a visit). Pe
 nn College offers a B.S. in Graphic Design with foundation courses in draw
 ing\, 2D and 3D design\, visual literacy\, painting\, ceramics\, wood scul
 pture\, printmaking\, and computer graphics\; artists are encouraged to pr
 opose class-specific programs and optional community workshops. Visiting a
 rtists give a 20-30 minute gallery talk during the public reception (held 
 Tuesdays through Thursdays). TO APPLY: 10-15 digital images of work (JPG/J
 PEG only\, max 4 MB each)\, an image list with dimensions and medium\, a C
 V\, and an artist statement. For two-person or group proposals\, provide d
 etails in the comments\; for video work\, upload a PDF with video links an
 d details. Deadline 31 August 2026 for the 2027-28 season.\n\nOrganisation
 : The Gallery at Penn College (Pennsylvania College of Technology)\nLocati
 on: Williamsport\, Pennsylvania\, United States (campus of Pennsylvania Co
 llege of Technology). Williamsport Regional Airport connects to Washington
 -Dulles\; no other direct flights or trains.\nAward: Housing\, meals\, a s
 olo exhibition honorarium\, and a shipping and travel stipend are provided
 . No application fee.\nDuration: Exhibitions scheduled during the 2027-28 
 season. Jurying takes place in the Fall 2026 semester\; artists are notifi
 ed in January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry
 -form?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.pct.edu/gallery/exhibitions-entry-form?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Williamsport\, Pennsylvania\, United States (campus of Pennsylvani
 a College of Technology). Williamsport Regional Airport connects to Washin
 gton-Dulles\; no other direct flights or trains.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:railyard-art-project-santa-fe-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
SUMMARY:Deadline: Railyard Art Project: Call for Temporary Public Art (Sant
 a Fe)
DESCRIPTION:The Railyard Art Project (RAP) facilitates temporary public art
  installations by established and emerging artists and arts collectives in
  Santa Fe's Railyard Park + Plaza. RAP is accepting proposals for temporar
 y public art installations and creative projects\; thanks to funding from 
 Santa Fe's Arts and Culture Commission\, two projects will be hosted betwe
 en October 2026 and June 2027\, each receiving a USD 3\,500 honorarium plu
 s marketing support and liability insurance. THEMES: the RAP Committee enc
 ourages collaboration across disciplines (art\, science\, history) and wel
 comes thoughtful\, challenging\, playful\, and edifying art in the context
  of social and cultural expression and environmental sustainability. Propo
 sed projects must physically withstand outdoor public display and engage t
 he community by responding to the natural and social landscape of the park
 . SELECTION CRITERIA: overall artistic merit\; context and familiarity wit
 h the proposed site\; practical viability\; and potential for community en
 gagement and cultural/educational enrichment. Artists should select an eli
 gible area of the park's open space and describe the project in detail\; p
 hotos or renderings may be uploaded with the application (a map of Railyar
 d Art Locations is linked from the program page). The RAP Committee\, made
  up of community members and Park + Plaza stakeholders\, selects and sched
 ules the exhibitions\; a formal in-person presentation may be requested de
 pending on complexity. CONDITIONS: the park operates under a Conservation 
 Easement\, so installations cannot be located on planted areas\, no stakin
 g of any kind is permitted (including during install/de-install)\, and not
 hing may be hung from trees. Artists lend artwork at their own risk (no pr
 otection against vandalism or weather damage\, and artists are responsible
  for repairs). Installs and de-installs must be coordinated in advance wit
 h the RAP Committee or Railyard Park Conservancy staff\, with a Committee 
 or staff member on site throughout. Application deadline 5 August 2026.\n\
 nOrganisation: Railyard Art Project (Railyard Park + Plaza)\nLocation: San
 ta Fe\, New Mexico\, United States (Railyard Park + Plaza\, City-owned pro
 perty under a Conservation Easement).\nAward: USD 3\,500 honorarium per pr
 oject (two projects selected)\, plus marketing support and liability insur
 ance. Funded by Santa Fe's Arts and Culture Commission. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Two projects hosted between October 2026 and June 2027.\n\nMo
 re: https://railyardpark.org/public-art-program/?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://railyardpark.org/public-art-program/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Santa Fe\, New Mexico\, United States (Railyard Park + Plaza\, Cit
 y-owned property under a Conservation Easement).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:target-margin-tmt-institute-fellowship-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: Target Margin Theater: TMT Institute Fellowship 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Target Margin Theater invites applications for its next cohort 
 of TMT Institute Fellows for the 2026/2027 season. The Institute is a coho
 rt of artists who seek to radically disrupt and re-examine their existing 
 practice without any goal or expected result: pure experimentation with no
  product-oriented end goal. Fellows pursue no project\, no developmental s
 tage\, no deliverable\; misfires\, dead ends\, and bad ideas are considere
 d a fruitful part of the work. SUPPORT: TMT provides space\, material reso
 urces\, collaborators\, and a USD 2\,000 stipend. STRUCTURE: three short i
 ntensive sessions at Target Margin Theater in Brooklyn (5-8 November 2026\
 , 8-10 January 2027\, and 19-21 March 2027\, all day with some evening hou
 rs)\, monthly breakfasts at Junior's in Brooklyn (20 October\, 30 November
 \, 4 January\, 1 February\, 8 March\, 5 April\, 3 May)\, and occasional ex
 cursions. After the second intensive\, each Fellow commits to an explorati
 on pursued outside group gatherings\, supported by TMT resources. The seas
 on ends with an Open Studio weekend in June 2027 to share explorations wit
 h the community\; this is not a performance and should not be an audience-
 attended\, time-bounded show. WHO CAN APPLY: artists from any discipline w
 ithin or outside theater\, as well as production\, technical\, and adminis
 trative workers in the arts. Applicants must have an established\, existin
 g body of work and practice they are committed to changing\; do not apply 
 with a project. Recent graduates of training programs are unlikely to be a
 ppropriate candidates\; no formal education level or degree is required. S
 ELECTION: TMT staff and artistic community review applications and invite 
 a handful of candidates to interview\; all applicants are notified by Octo
 ber. Deadline: Wednesday 22 July 2026\, 11:59 pm EST.\n\nOrganisation: Tar
 get Margin Theater\nLocation: Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset 
 Park). Fellows must be available in person for intensives\, monthly breakf
 asts\, and excursions.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend\, plus space\, material 
 resources\, and collaborators to support each Fellow's experiments. No app
 lication fee.\nDuration: 2026-27 season: three intensive sessions in Brook
 lyn (5-8 November 2026\, 8-10 January 2027\, 19-21 March 2027)\, monthly b
 reakfasts October 2026 through May 2027\, and an Open Studio weekend in Ju
 ne 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.targetmargin.org/institute/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York\, United States (Sunset Park). Fellows must be
  available in person for intensives\, monthly breakfasts\, and excursions.
END:VEVENT
