UID:k3-tanzplan-hamburg-choreography-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: K3 | Tanzplan Hamburg: Choreography Residency 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:K3 in Hamburg is one of the better-funded choreography residenc
 ies in Europe\, and it is aimed squarely at people who want research time 
 rather than a production deadline. Three choreographers are selected for e
 ight months at Kampnagel with a monthly scholarship\, studio access\, a pr
 oduction budget\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support\, plus outr
 each work and a public presentation at the end. Selection runs in two stag
 es. ELIGIBILITY: individual choreographers at an emerging to early mid-car
 eer stage who have already produced independent choreographic work outside
  an educational context. Open internationally\; formal dance training is n
 ot required. Teams and ensembles are not eligible\, only individuals. Appl
 ications close 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: K3 | Tanzpla
 n Hamburg (Kampnagel)\nLocation: Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.\
 nAward: Monthly scholarship (EUR 2\,500 per month in the 2025/26 cycle\; t
 he 2027/28 figure had not been announced at the time of listing)\, plus on
 e-time travel expenses\, visa assistance\, a moderate production budget\, 
 studio access\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support. No applicati
 on fee is stated.\nDuration: Eight months\, August 2027 to April 2028\n\nM
 ore: https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-fears-berlin-performance-art-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEW FEARS Gallery: Berlin Performance Art Award 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEW FEARS is a Berlin gallery set up as a hybrid space for arti
 sts working across choreography and visual art\, with a focus on body-base
 d practice\, and this award extends that to artists working outside conven
 tional theatre and institutional structures. A jury of Bryana Fritz\, Jaco
 po Lanteri and Burcu Bilgic shortlists six projects\, each of which perfor
 ms live at the gallery on 16 or 17 October 2026\; three winners are then c
 hosen and celebrated at Panke Club on 18 October. The application is unusu
 ally light: name\, email\, an optional text of up to 500 words about your 
 work\, and up to six links to video documentation with passwords where nee
 ded. No CV\, no written proposal\, no budget\, which means the video docum
 entation carries the whole application\, so lead with work that shows imme
 diately how it operates live in a gallery. ELIGIBILITY: Berlin-based artis
 ts\, duos and groups working across performance\, choreography\, visual ar
 t or body-based practice. You must be in Berlin for setup the week before 
 and for the presentations on 16-17 October 2026\, and be able to issue an 
 invoice for the award payment. Existing or new work is fine\, with no stat
 ed duration limits. AVAILABLE EQUIPMENT: sound system\, speakers\, mixer\,
  LED lights\, projector\, wind machine\, smoke machine\, microphone and ba
 sic cables\; check the venue PDF before proposing anything that depends on
  a particular room\, access route or technical setup. NOTE: the organisers
  describe this as a DIY\, near-zero-budget initiative. There is no product
 ion budget and nothing beyond the grant\, so a group splitting EUR 1\,300 
 should go in with eyes open. TIMELINE: applications close 15 August 2026 (
 no time or timezone given)\; shortlist announced 15 September 2026. Apply 
 through the JotForm at form.jotform.com/261674665583066.\n\nOrganisation: 
 NEW FEARS Gallery\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichen
 bergerstrasse 114\, 10999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.\nAward: T
 hree winning projects each receive a EUR 1\,300 grant\, shared between the
  members of a duo or group\, plus a ten-day residency at NEW FEARS Gallery
 \, basic technical support and access to the gallery's equipment. The thre
 e shortlisted projects that do not win divide 50 percent of the entrance r
 evenue equally. Travel\, accommodation\, materials\, shipping\, insurance\
 , per diem and production costs are NOT covered\, and artists with a non-G
 erman tax number may see tax deducted from the prize at an unstated rate. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-day residency scheduled with the winner
 s during 2026 or 2027\; setup the week before 16 October\, public performa
 nces 16-17 October 2026\n\nMore: https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichenbergerstrasse 114\, 1
 0999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:drift-choreographer-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: DRIFT: Choreographer Open Call 2026-27 (Dance and Researc
 h In The Future Time)
DESCRIPTION:DRIFT (Dance and Research In The Future Time) is a four-year Cr
 eative Europe cooperation between ten European organisations\, led by Thea
 tre de Liege and Maison de la danse. The distinguishing feature is that it
  funds artistic research rather than production: you are expected to arriv
 e with questions\, not a finished piece you already intend to make\, and r
 eviewers read every section of the application through that lens. STRUCTUR
 E: each of the ten partner organisations selects one choreographer from it
 s own country\, forming a cohort of ten. Over the programme you get an Onb
 oarding Lab\, two weeks of residency with your national partner\, three co
 llaborative Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic an
 d curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress showcase at the Biennale d
 e la danse in Lyon\, all backed by a EUR 20\,000 research budget that cove
 rs your fees\, travel\, accommodation and any collaborators. SECOND PHASE:
  after a further evaluation\, five of the ten artists may receive co-produ
 ction support and touring across the DRIFT network. That phase is separate
  from this application and is not guaranteed. ELIGIBILITY: professional ch
 oreographers in contemporary dance or performance\, at a mid-career stage\
 , meaning an established national practice but not yet fully established i
 nternationally. You must be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin re
 gion only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portug
 al\, and you apply through the partner organisation representing your coun
 try of residence\, not centrally. Good English and availability for all pr
 ogramme dates are required. The country list is strict\, so check it befor
 e you start. TIMELINE: applications opened 1 July 2026 and close 31 August
  2026\; selection through September\; results 9 October 2026\; programme r
 uns November 2026 to September 2027.\n\nOrganisation: DRIFT\, a four-year 
 Creative Europe cooperation of ten organisations led by Theatre de Liege a
 nd Maison de la danse\nLocation: Activities across Europe: kick-off in Ber
 lin\, Festival Lab in Tunis\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a resi
 dency hosted by your own national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based 
 in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Ita
 ly\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner
  organisation for their country of residence.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 research
  budget per choreographer\, covering artistic fees\, travel\, accommodatio
 n and optional collaborator costs. Also included: an Onboarding Lab\, two 
 weeks of residency with your national DRIFT partner\, three collaborative 
 Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic mentoring and 
 individual curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress presentation at t
 he Biennale de la danse in Lyon. No application fee.\nDuration: November 2
 026 to September 2027. Kick-off in Berlin 30 November to 1 December 2026\;
  Festival Lab in Tunis 18-21 March 2027\; Biennale de la danse showcase in
  Lyon 18-25 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-ca
 ll?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities across Europe: kick-off in Berlin\, Festival Lab in Tun
 is\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a residency hosted by your own 
 national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based in Belgium\, France\, Ger
 many (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tu
 nisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner organisation for their c
 ountry of residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:liveline-fund-round-1-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260818
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260819
SUMMARY:Deadline: Liveline Fund: Round 1 (UK grassroots live music)
DESCRIPTION:Liveline is a national risk-sharing fund rebuilding grassroots 
 touring\, programming and local music scenes across the UK\, delivered by 
 Music Venue Trust with Save Our Scene and the Association of Independent P
 romoters\, alongside the LIVE Trust and the industry's voluntary levy on s
 tadium and arena tickets. The premise is that good shows are not happening
  because the downside risk is too high\, so rather than a traditional subs
 idy it is an incentive-led model that only pays out when things go wrong. 
 FOUR FUNDS: (1) GUARANTORSHIP SCHEME covers up to 80 percent of losses on 
 an individual show or tour\, for promoters\, in-house bookers\, artists or
  their representatives\; break even and nothing is paid\, turn a profit an
 d you keep it. (2) LOCAL SCENE DEVELOPMENT FUND covers venue hire costs an
 d local promoter training to build regular programming\, for independent p
 romoters or venues developing an in-house programme. (3) TOUR EXTENSION AN
 D REGIONAL ACCESS FUND covers the long travel\, fuel\, accommodation and f
 erry costs that stop tours reaching remote parts of the UK\, for promoters
 \, agents\, management and artists. (4) GENRE SPECIFIC SAFETY AND ACCESS F
 UND covers extra security\, licensing and structural costs for genres and 
 scenes that face additional barriers\, for venues\, promoters\, artists or
  collectives. ELIGIBILITY: grassroots venues (Music Venue Alliance member 
 venues)\, independent promoters including AIP members\, in-house venue pro
 grammers\, and artists\, agents and teams building sustainable tours. Guar
 antorship shows must be at MVA subscribed venues\, and for a full tour at 
 least 70 percent of dates must be at subscribed MVT venues. TWO CATCHES WO
 RTH KNOWING: tours must NOT already be announced\, and Liveline funds a he
 adline artist only once. Applications for Round 1 close Tuesday 18 August 
 2026. Start with the eligibility checker at livelinefund.uk\, or contact i
 nfo@livelinefund.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Liveline\, delivered by Music Venue 
 Trust with Save Our Scene and the Association of Independent Promoters\nLo
 cation: United Kingdom. Guarantorship shows must be at Music Venue Allianc
 e member venues\; for a full tour at least 70 percent of dates must be at 
 subscribed MVT venues. Regional focus includes the Highlands and Islands\,
  West Wales and Northern Ireland.\nAward: No fixed grant sizes published\;
  every application is assessed case by case against a real budget rather t
 han first come first served. The Guarantorship Scheme covers up to 80 perc
 ent of losses on a show or tour: if it breaks even nothing is paid\, and i
 f it turns a profit the promoter or venue keeps it. The other three funds 
 cover venue hire\, travel\, accommodation\, ferry and remote-access costs\
 , or genre-specific safety\, licensing and access costs. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Round 1\; applications close Tuesday 18 August 2026\n\nMore:
  https://livelinefund.uk/funding-overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://livelinefund.uk/funding-overview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom. Guarantorship shows must be at Music Venue Allianc
 e member venues\; for a full tour at least 70 percent of dates must be at 
 subscribed MVT venues. Regional focus includes the Highlands and Islands\,
  West Wales and Northern Ireland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arts-council-england-music-growth-fund-small-strand-2026@artificialnouv
 eau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts Council England: Music Growth Fund\, GBP 1\,000-40\,
 000 strand
DESCRIPTION:The Music Growth Fund is Arts Council England's new investment 
 programme for growth in new music\, meaning original live performance\, so
 und recording or musical work recently or newly created or performed by li
 ving UK-based artists and music creators. It sits inside the wider GBP 45 
 million Music Growth Package. WHO IT IS FOR: music festivals\, music manag
 ers\, music promoters\, music publishers\, record labels\, recording studi
 os\, rehearsal spaces and grassroots music venues\, plus established secto
 r bodies and other sector support organisations that support them. Each ap
 plicant type has its own Applicant Specific Guidance on the Music Growth P
 ackage pages. IMPORTANT: this is business and organisational investment\, 
 not a grant for individual artists to make work. Individual artists\, band
 s\, producers and artist collectives should instead look at the separate M
 usic Creators Growth Fund (GBP 10\,000-20\,000)\, whose guidance lands 1 S
 eptember 2026 with the portal opening 1 October 2026. WHAT TO PROPOSE: how
  investment will be used to grow music businesses and/or the wider music i
 ndustry\, and enhance opportunities for artists and music creators\, indus
 try professionals\, music fans and new audiences. Growth can mean increase
 d reach\, stronger business models\, improved sustainability\, skills deve
 lopment or expanded capacity\, and is judged in the context of each applic
 ant's own scale and ambitions. DECISION CRITERIA: potential for growth\, e
 ffective use of funds\, contribution to a fairer and more inclusive music 
 industry\, and contribution to a climate resilient music industry. BEFORE 
 YOU START: you need an approved profile on the Arts Council grants managem
 ent system before you can submit\, so allow time for that. Arts Council re
 serves the right to change the programme\, so check you are on the latest 
 guidance. THIS STRAND: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, with a GBP 3\,000\,000 
 annual budget for 2026/27 and a 25 percent cash match requirement. ROUNDS 
 FOR 2026/27 (GBP 1\,000-40\,000 strand): Round 1 opens 6 August and closes
  3 September 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 2 opens 4 September and clo
 ses 15 October 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 3 opens 16 October and cl
 oses 26 November 2026\, decision 3 February 2027. Round 4 opens 27 Novembe
 r 2026 and closes 7 January 2027\, decision 9 March 2027. ROUNDS FOR THE G
 BP 40\,001-100\,000 strand: Round 1 opens 7 September and closes 1 October
  2026\, decision 30 November. Round 2 opens 23 November 2026 and closes 15
  January 2027\, decision 8 March 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Arts Council Engla
 nd\nLocation: England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and
  music creators.\nAward: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, from a total annual b
 udget of GBP 3\,000\,000 for this strand in 2026/27. You must find at leas
 t 25 percent of the total project budget as CASH match funding. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: Four rounds across 2026/27\; the next closes 3 Septem
 ber 2026\n\nMore: https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/musi
 c-growth-fund-general-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/music-growth-fund-g
 eneral-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and music
  creators.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:youth-music-trailblazer-fund-round-13-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Youth Music: Trailblazer Fund (Round 13)
DESCRIPTION:Youth Music's Trailblazer Fund gives GBP 2\,000 to GBP 30\,000 
 to organisations in England running projects that let children and young p
 eople aged 25 or under make\, learn and earn in music. A project should do
  at least one of three things: trial work or test a new way of working\, s
 ustain a grassroots programme\, or disrupt the status quo. Doing all three
  is welcome. THEMES: your work must meet one of these: early years\; Disab
 led\, d/Deaf and neurodivergent young people\; the youth justice system\; 
 young people facing barriers\; young adults\; or organisations and the wor
 kforce. WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE\, using Youth Music's own examples:
  a new CIC testing ways to help nurseries embed music-making into the day\
 ; a small grassroots music organisation sustaining a programme that alread
 y works\; a Music Education Hub setting up a Youth Board with real budget 
 and decision-making power over how the hub is run\; or an established yout
 h centre trying a music programme for the first time. ROUND 13: opened 31 
 July 2026\, deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\, notification 11 December 2026
 \, for projects starting between January and March 2027. ROUND 14: opens 2
 3 October 2026\, deadline 20 November 2026 at 5pm\, notification 19 March 
 2027\, for projects starting between April and June 2027. All deadlines ar
 e 5pm. NOTE: this is a grant to ORGANISATIONS\, not to individual musician
 s or composers.\n\nOrganisation: Youth Music\nLocation: England only. Proj
 ects for children and young people aged 25 or under.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to
  GBP 30\,000 for organisations in England. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Round 13 projects start between January and March 2027. Round opened 31 Ju
 ly 2026\; deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\; notification 11 December 2026.\
 n\nMore: https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-
 fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-fund?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England only. Projects for children and young people aged 25 or un
 der.
END:VEVENT
