UID:dot-line-space-water-bodies-ecology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dot Line Space Art Foundation: Water Bodies & Ecology 202
 6 (Mumbai)
DESCRIPTION:Water Bodies & Ecology 2026 is a 40-day multidisciplinary initi
 ative from the Dot Line Space Art Foundation in Mumbai\, bringing artists\
 , researchers\, collectives and communities together across research activ
 ities\, installations and exhibitions at two venues. The premise is artist
 ic engagement with water\, ecology\, climate\, memory and environmental co
 nsciousness\, and the programme is explicitly after research-based and sit
 e-responsive approaches rather than finished gallery objects\, aiming for 
 dialogue between contemporary practice and ecological concerns. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: individual artists\, artist groups\, interdisciplinary practitioners\
 , community-based initiatives\, and collectives and collaborative practice
 s\, working across all mediums and disciplines. WHAT IS OFFERED: selected 
 artists receive grants towards travel and production expenses. Be aware th
 at the published call does not give amounts\, does not mention an artist f
 ee\, and does not mention accommodation\, so confirm the terms with the fo
 undation (info@dotlinespace.co.in) before committing to travel. Applicatio
 ns go through a Google Form linked from the project page. ABOUT THE HOST: 
 Dot Line Space was co-founded in 2015 by Gourmoni Das\, Piyush Bavalia and
  Shruti Bhosle\, all graduates of Sir J.J. School of Art in Mumbai.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Dot Line Space Art Foundation\nLocation: Mumbai\, India. Presen
 ted across two venues\; the foundation operates out of the New Great Easte
 rn Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fish Art Gallery. Open to artists
  internationally.\nAward: Grants towards travel and production expenses fo
 r selected artists. The call does not publish fixed amounts and does not s
 tate an artist fee or accommodation. No application fee.\nDuration: A 40-d
 ay multidisciplinary programme in 2026 across two Mumbai venues. Deadline 
 30 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-e
 cology-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.dotlinespace.co.in/water-bodies-and-ecology-2026/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Mumbai\, India. Presented across two venues\; the foundation opera
 tes out of the New Great Eastern Mills in Byculla\, where it runs Nine Fis
 h Art Gallery. Open to artists internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:julies-bicycle-iter-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ITER 2026: International Touring and Environmental Respon
 sibility Programme
DESCRIPTION:ITER is now in its fifth year and supports people in the perfor
 ming arts to build environmentally responsible\, inclusive and equitable w
 ays of touring and collaborating internationally. It is initiated and fina
 nced by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Creative Scotland and
  the Danish Arts Foundation\, and designed and delivered by Julie's Bicycl
 e and in futurum. Creative Scotland joined as a partner for 2026. TWO TRAC
 KS\, up to 60 places in total: PATHFINDERS\, ten sessions introducing the 
 principles of sustainable touring for people new to the subject\; and CHAN
 GEMAKERS\, nine sessions for those further along\, on leadership and syste
 mic change. Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 December 2026\, Pathf
 inders on Tuesdays and Changemakers on Thursdays\, 10:00 to 12:00 UK time\
 , alongside networking and a new alumni network. THE MONEY COMES IN PHASE 
 TWO: after the seminars\, groups of three or more participants can apply f
 or up to GBP 30\,000 to implement collaborative projects that cut environm
 ental impacts and advance climate justice. Funding applications open 6 Nov
 ember 2026 and close 11 January 2027\; awarded projects begin in spring 20
 27. ELIGIBILITY: based in England\, Scotland\, Norway or Denmark\, working
  in dance\, music or theatre. That covers music and theatre venues receivi
 ng touring work\, festivals\, orchestras\, theatre companies\, solo artist
 s\, performers and musicians\, performing arts producers\, and multidiscip
 linary arts organisations. Applications are made through the relevant nati
 onal funder (Arts Council England open funds\, Creative Scotland targeted 
 funding\, Arts Council Norway\, Danish Arts Foundation). Questions go to e
 nvironmentaltouring@artscouncil.org.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Julie's Bicycle a
 nd in futurum\, funded by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Cre
 ative Scotland and the Danish Arts Foundation\nLocation: England\, Scotlan
 d\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.\nAward: The seminar programme
  is free to join. Groups of three or more participants can then apply for 
 up to GBP 30\,000 for collaborative projects\, with funding applications o
 pen 6 November 2026 to 11 January 2027 and projects starting spring 2027. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 De
 cember 2026\, Tuesdays for Pathfinders and Thursdays for Changemakers\, 10
 :00 to 12:00 UK time. Deadline to join 1 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://
 juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England\, Scotland\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foodculture-days-kitchen-central-location-2027@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Kitchen / Central Location Open Call (foodculture day
 s Biennale\, Vevey 2027)
DESCRIPTION:foodculture days is opening up The Kitchen / Central Location\,
  the living heart of its biennale in Vevey\, to an outside collective for 
 the festival's 6th edition. The Kitchen is at once an open-air kitchen\, a
  gathering space and an artistic installation\, sited by the lake on the E
 splanade de la Paix along a busy promenade\, hosting multidisciplinary pro
 jects\, concerts\, themed meals\, public discussions\, citizens' assemblie
 s and moments of collective cooking. The call asks for new ways of inhabit
 ing it. WHO THEY WANT: a transdisciplinary collective of scientists\, arti
 sts\, designers\, craftspeople\, cooks\, activists and architects\, either
  newly formed or already active\; collaboration across generations\, cultu
 res and levels of expertise is encouraged. WHAT IS OFFERED: an open-air ci
 tizen experimentation laboratory in Vevey's public space\, framed around r
 ethinking ways of living and the relationship to time and to creation\, an
 d thinking together about the conditions under which multidisciplinary pra
 ctices can flourish. THE BRIEF: kitchen and hospitality\, lunch and dinner
  service\, vegetarian meals with a vegan option. Meals should be affordabl
 e\, seasonal where fresh produce is involved\, and conceived in the spirit
  of a communal canteen: simple\, generous\, nourishing and full of flavour
 . Expected covers are roughly 100 guests Wednesday to Thursday and roughly
  150 Friday to Sunday. WHAT THE FESTIVAL PROVIDES: curatorial support to d
 evelop the proposal\, with particular attention to the themed meals\; intr
 oductions to local producers\, growers and artisans including farm visits 
 and foraging sites\; production support and volunteers throughout\; and st
 andard professional kitchen equipment and cooking facilities. BUDGET: CHF 
 15\,000 all-inclusive (VAT included) covering travel\, visas\, equipment r
 ental\, any additional kitchen equipment\, per diems\, materials and artis
 t fees\; food costs and accommodation in Vevey are covered separately. THE
  SPACE: the pavilion\, called Cuisine\, is a modular metal structure assem
 bled from repurposed agricultural greenhouse components on a 3.1 metre bid
 irectional grid\, with pillars and arches forming ridge vaults\, a textile
  covering naturally dyed yellow with reseda\, and a terracotta brick heart
 h for wood-fired cooking laid directly on the floor. It comes with dining 
 tables\, low tables\, a reception table\, a shelf\, a bar\, pendant lights
 \, 30 cushions\, removable curtains\, flags\, blackboards and signage pane
 ls\, and can expand\, contract or be reconfigured. TIMELINE: Phase 1 deadl
 ine 31 August 2026 at midnight CET\; three shortlisted proposals selected 
 in late September\, each invited to an online meeting and paid a CHF 250 r
 esearch fee for Phase 2\; final project selected and announced in early Oc
 tober 2026\; collaborative development with the foodculture days architect
 ural team October to November\; production December to February\; build an
 d installation in Vevey 1 to 16 June 2027\, with the collective present th
 roughout those two weeks\; 6th edition of the biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
  PHASE 1 QUESTIONS: tell us about your collective (origins\, intention\, w
 hat makes you special)\; member biographies with collective or individual 
 portfolios\, website\, Instagram\, city of residence and practices\; what 
 the concept consists of in terms of food and cuisine\, relationships\, and
  scenography and spatial research\; what you want to put into practice in 
 the central pavilion\, your vision and the kind of experience you want to 
 create\; any special requirements\; and references\, including a quote rep
 resenting the collective\, two reference texts\, three images and optional
 ly video. An Open Call Information Kit\, a recording of the online informa
 tion session and a Q&A are published on the open call site and are worth r
 eading before applying.\n\nOrganisation: foodculture days\nLocation: Vevey
 \, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la Paix by the la
 keside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for the two-week 
 build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the biennale 16 t
 o 20 June 2027.\nAward: CHF 15\,000 all-inclusive project budget (VAT incl
 uded)\, covering travel\, visas\, equipment and additional kitchen equipme
 nt rental\, per diems\, materials and artist fees. Food costs and accommod
 ation in Vevey are covered separately by foodculture days. Three shortlist
 ed collectives each receive a CHF 250 research fee to develop their propos
 al in Phase 2. One collective is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 October 2026 to June 2027: collaborative development October-November\, pr
 oduction December-February\, on-site build 1-16 June 2027\, biennale 16-20
  June 2027\n\nMore: https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://opencall.foodculturedays.com/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Vevey\, Switzerland. The Kitchen is built on the Esplanade de la P
 aix by the lakeside. The selected collective must be present in Vevey for 
 the two-week build and installation\, 1 to 16 June 2027\, and through the 
 biennale 16 to 20 June 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:teatri-riflessi-2027-short-performance-competition@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Teatri Riflessi 2027: Short Performance Competition (12th
  edition)
DESCRIPTION:An open call for at least 10 short performances of maximum 15 m
 inutes for the 12th edition of Teatri Riflessi in Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily
 . THE FORMAT: the competition champions the short performance in its own r
 ight\, not as a reduction of a longer work nor as a sketch for a full-even
 ing adaptation\; its strength is the artistic immediacy and urgency it car
 ries. The framing connects ancient Greek theatre\, conceived as an outdoor
  competition for a large audience\, to the more provocative tendencies of 
 happenings and performance art that lean on the beholder's desire to parti
 cipate. STRUCTURE: semi-finalists perform on the evenings of Thursday 15 a
 nd Friday 16 July 2027\; the Programmers' Panel and the Scholars' & Critic
 s' Panel determine the finalists\, who perform again in the final on Sunda
 y 18 July. The panels bring together around 30 Italian and international a
 rtistic directors\, curators\, festival and theatre programmers\, journali
 sts\, critics and performing arts scholars\, many from the Teatri Riflessi
  Network and Anticorpi XL Network\; the full list is published in June 202
 7. Past partner organisations include Antistatic Festival (Sofia)\, ATER F
 ondazione\, BIG Festival\, Cantieri Danza\, Circuito CLAPS\, COORPI\, Danc
 e ICONS (Washington DC)\, Danza Urbana\, Derida Dance Centre\, EN-KNAP and
  Spanski Borci (Ljubljana)\, Fabbrica Europa\, Festival Ammutinamenti\, Cl
 uny Danse (France)\, Danza Canarias dentro y fuera (Tenerife)\, FIND Festi
 val\, Fondazione Armunia and Festival Inequilibrio\, Fondazione Claudia Lo
 mbardi per il teatro (Lugano)\, Fondazione Sipario Toscana\, Fondazione To
 scana Spettacolo\, Galway Dance (Ireland)\, Interplay Festival (Turin)\, L
 'Arboreto Teatro Dimora\, La Piccionaia\, Movimento Danza\, New Dance For 
 Asia International Festival (Daegu)\, Puglia Culture\, Toplocentrala (Sofi
 a) and Umbria Danza Festival. MONEY: each selected performance receives a 
 gross fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1\,100 depending on cast size\, plus a possibl
 e travel contribution of up to EUR 250. Awards are Best Performance EUR 1\
 ,200 (from the combined weighted scores of both panels\, the Jury of the F
 inal\, the Youth Jury and the Citizens' Jury)\, Best Dramaturgy EUR 500 an
 d the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 (both from the Scholars' & Crit
 ics' Panel)\, Best Direction EUR 500 and Best Performer EUR 500 (both from
  the Programmers' Panel)\, the 'Valentina Nicosia' Fund of EUR 500 (joint 
 decision of both panels)\, the 'Sandra Bressan' research fund of EUR 1\,80
 0 (awarded by the Citizens' Jury to reinvite a company to Sicily to work w
 ith the community)\, and the Youth Jury Award of EUR 300 to a semi-finalis
 t\, alongside the Audience Special Mention\, the Companies' Choice Award a
 nd the Technical Crew Award. Fees\, reimbursements and prizes are paid aft
 er the festival\, provided all regulations and deadlines are met. FEES TO 
 APPLY: none. Only selected companies pay a EUR 50 deposit on selection\, f
 ully refunded after the festival. RULES: performances should aim for 15 mi
 nutes\, with total stage occupancy including set-up and dismantling capped
  at 20 minutes. Multiple entries are allowed but need a separate form each
 . No major changes are permitted after selection\; minor adjustments\, esp
 ecially cast substitutions\, must be communicated in advance and authorise
 d by IterCulture APS. Works in languages other than Italian are welcome an
 d are presented with Italian and English surtitles prepared by IterCulture
  APS. APPLICATION: a digital form linked in Article 5 of the open call reg
 ulations\, with Annex A for dramaturgy (the complete script where one exis
 ts\, or for dance\, physical theatre and non-text work a short document on
  the dramaturgical research and choreographic structure) and a full video 
 recording of the complete work\; excerpts and teasers are not accepted\, a
 nd for rehearsed readings\, directorial notes on costumes\, lighting and s
 et design are mandatory. COMMITMENT: rehearsal attendance is compulsory\, 
 with the day assigned by the Competition Manager after selection\; compani
 es must arrive in Zafferana Etnea by their rehearsal day (rehearsals run 1
 3 and 14 July 2027) and stay until the festival closes at 23:30 on Sunday 
 18 July. Rehearsals and public events run mainly in afternoons and evening
 s\; the only compulsory morning commitment is the panel feedback session o
 n Sunday 18 July. Online information sessions are held on Google Meet on T
 uesday 22 September 2026\, with the English session at 19:30 CEST. Deadlin
 e 15 October 2026 at 5pm CET.\n\nOrganisation: IterCulture APS\nLocation: 
 Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and international compan
 ies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2027\; re
 hearsals 13 and 14 July.\nAward: Gross performance fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1
 \,100 (VAT included) per selected short performance\, scaled by the number
  of performers on stage\, plus a possible travel contribution of up to EUR
  250 subject to available funding\, place of origin\, cast size and absenc
 e of external touring grants. At least 10 performances are selected. Compe
 tition prizes: Best Performance EUR 1\,200\; Best Dramaturgy\, Best Direct
 ion\, Best Performer and the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 each\; '
 Valentina Nicosia' Fund EUR 500\; 'Sandra Bressan' research fund EUR 1\,80
 0\; Youth Jury Award EUR 300\; plus non-cash awards (Audience Special Ment
 ion\, Companies' Choice\, Technical Crew). Panel members and Teatri Rifles
 si Network partners may also award special prizes in the form of artist re
 sidencies and touring opportunities. Application is free\; selected compan
 ies pay a EUR 50 deposit that is fully refunded after the festival.\nDurat
 ion: Festival participation 13 to 18 July 2027\, with mandatory rehearsals
  and presence until 23:30 on 18 July\n\nMore: https://www.iterculture.eu/e
 n/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.iterculture.eu/en/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and internationa
 l companies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2
 027\; rehearsals 13 and 14 July.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dp-curator-support-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: 2027 d/p Curator Support Open Call (Seoul)
DESCRIPTION:d/p is an independent alternative exhibition space in the Nakwo
 n Arcade\, the historic mixed-use commercial and residential complex in Jo
 ngno-gu\, Seoul built in 1968. Its Curator Support programme\, sponsored b
 y NakwonSangga\, has selected two to three independent curators every year
  since 2018\, giving each an exhibition venue\, curatorial collaboration w
 ith the d/p team\, and a modest curatorial grant to develop and realise th
 eir curatorial practice. ELIGIBILITY: individual curators or teams with at
  least one curating experience within the past three years. The applying c
 urator or team cannot participate in the exhibition as an artist\; curator
 s and artists must be clearly distinguished. SELECTION: approximately two 
 individuals or teams. WHAT IS PROVIDED: a curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,
 000 including tax\; use of the d/p exhibition space of approximately 187 s
 quare metres\; and a five-week exhibition slot somewhere between March and
  December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation time counted separat
 ely from the five weeks. PROCESS: first round is a document review of the 
 APPLICATION and PROPOSAL submitted in the provided format\; second round i
 s individual interviews followed by final selection. Interviews are conduc
 ted offline in principle\, but may be held online if the applicant is abro
 ad or in other exceptional circumstances. HOW TO APPLY: submit the APPLICA
 TION via the Google Form and the PROPOSAL by email to dslashp@gmail.com be
 tween 3 August and 30 September 2026. The proposal template is downloadabl
 e from the link in the @dslashp Instagram bio. File name format: '2027 d/p
  Curator Support Application_Applicant Name'. Submit as PDF\; if the propo
 sal includes video\, provide a link rather than a file. KEY DATES: submiss
 ions 3 August to 30 September 2026\; first round results announced in the 
 first week of October 2026 by individual contact\; second round interviews
  in the second week of October 2026\; final selection announced in the thi
 rd week of October 2026 by individual contact and on the @dslashp account.
  Inquiries: Minji Lee\, Curator\, dslashp@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: d/p\
 , sponsored by NakwonSangga\nLocation: d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil
 -daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, South Korea. Interviews are held offline in p
 rinciple\, with online interviews possible for applicants abroad or in oth
 er exceptional circumstances.\nAward: Curatorial grant of KRW 4\,000\,000 
 (including tax) per selected curator or team\, plus use of d/p's approxima
 tely 187 square metre exhibition space for a five-week exhibition and cura
 torial collaboration with the d/p team. Approximately two curators or team
 s are selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Five-week exhibition slot b
 etween March and December 2027\, with installation and deinstallation peri
 ods separate\n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NC
 ePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcjNNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1iSjEuCjay7DhL1AF1NCePu-5RV3t_10WT0CNcj
 NNJy8/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:d/p\, 417 Nakwon Arcade\, 428 Samil-daero\, Jongno-gu\, Seoul\, So
 uth Korea. Interviews are held offline in principle\, with online intervie
 ws possible for applicants abroad or in other exceptional circumstances.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mpi-empirical-aesthetics-inhabit-16-17-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: INHABIT #16 and #17: Artist-in-Residence 2027 (Max Planck
  Institute for Empirical Aesthetics)
DESCRIPTION:INHABIT is the artist-in-residence programme of the Max Planck 
 Institute for Empirical Aesthetics in Frankfurt am Main\, which invites ar
 tists from various disciplines to collaborate with its scientists and rese
 archers\, treating artistic thinking and knowledge as reflective practices
  in their own right. The 2027 call is addressed to artists from the perfor
 ming arts and music\, who spend four months creating new work\, or develop
 ing an existing project\, inside a scientific research environment and in 
 dialogue with the institute's researchers. Two slots are open for 2027: IN
 HABIT #16 from April to July 2027 and INHABIT #17 from September to Decemb
 er 2027. Work developed during the residency is presented in cooperation w
 ith local arts institutions as an exhibition\, performance\, concert or ot
 her format\, complemented by discursive formats. WHO THEY ARE LOOKING FOR:
  artists from the performing arts and music interested in interdisciplinar
 y collaboration between artistic and scientific fields\; artists open to d
 eveloping and sharing a project as an exhibition\, performance\, concert o
 r another format\; and artists active in contemporary culture who respond 
 to the institute with innovative and challenging ideas. WHAT IS OFFERED: a
 n artist's fee of EUR 8\,000\, an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a proj
 ect-dependent production budget\, travel costs to and in Frankfurt\, reali
 sation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access to t
 he library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and curatorial suppo
 rt during the residency. APPLY through the institute's e-recruiting portal
  by 1 September 2026\, 23:59 CET\; the jury decision follows in November 2
 026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Max Planck Institute for Empirical Aesthetic
 s\nLocation: Frankfurt am Main\, Germany\nAward: Artist's fee of EUR 8\,00
 0 plus an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a production budget depending 
 on the project\, and travel costs to and within Frankfurt. Also included: 
 realisation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access
  to the institute's library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and
  curatorial support throughout the residency. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Four months. Two slots: INHABIT #16 runs April to July 2027\, INHABIT #
 17 runs September to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/
 en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.h
 tml?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Frankfurt am Main\, Germany
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tartu-city-of-literature-residency-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tartu UNESCO City of Literature Residency for Writers and
  Translators\, Autumn 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Estonian Literary Society\, which coordinates Tartu UNESCO 
 City of Literature\, runs an international residency programme for writers
  and translators that has hosted authors from Slovenia\, Ireland\, Hungary
 \, the USA\, the UK\, Ukraine and Canada since 2017. This is the open call
  for the autumn 2026 slot: one resident stays two months in the Karl Risti
 kivi residency apartment in Tartu\, working on their own writing or transl
 ation while connecting with the local literary scene. WHAT THE RESIDENCY O
 FFERS: a EUR 600 monthly stipend\, travel reimbursement up to EUR 350\, fr
 ee accommodation\, a local support coordinator to help arrange cultural en
 gagements\, and opportunities to perform and present at literary events. T
 he autumn period covers the interdisciplinary literary festival Crazy Tart
 u on 12 to 14 November\, and the resident is offered a slot to perform the
 re alongside writers\, poets\, musicians and artists from Estonia and abro
 ad. ELIGIBILITY: open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian liter
 ature of any nationality\; applicants need an upper-intermediate level of 
 spoken English\, at least one published book\, screenplay\, theatre script
  or translation from Estonian into another language\, a demonstrable inter
 est in Estonian culture and literature\, a willingness to take part in loc
 al literary activities\, and a commitment to working on their own literary
  project during the stay. HOW TO APPLY: complete the online application fo
 rm linked from the residency page and upload a fragment of up to five page
 s of a published text (in Estonian or English) or of a published translati
 on. SELECTION: made by a committee representing the Estonian Literary Soci
 ety\, the Estonian Writers Union\, the Estonian Literary Museum and the Ta
 rtu Literature House Foundation. Results are announced before 15 September
  2026. The programme's stated aims are to enhance international exchange\,
  diversify the local literary scene\, introduce new foreign authors and th
 eir work to Estonian readers\, and build lasting connections beyond the re
 sidency period. Questions: Marja Unt\, eks@kirjandus.ee. Deadline 1 Septem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Estonian Literary Society (Eesti Kirjanduse Sel
 ts)\, Focal Point for Tartu UNESCO City of Literature\nLocation: Tartu\, E
 stonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian literature wor
 ldwide.\nAward: Monthly stipend of EUR 600 for the two-month stay (EUR 1\,
 200 in total)\, plus reimbursement of travel costs up to EUR 350. Free acc
 ommodation is provided in the Karl Ristikivi residency apartment\, which h
 as a bedroom\, living room\, kitchen\, bathroom and exhibition room. One r
 esident is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, 15 Octobe
 r to 15 December 2026\, with some flexibility on exact dates\n\nMore: http
 s://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tartu\, Estonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estoni
 an literature worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:this-is-gender-regional-curatorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260922
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260923
SUMMARY:Deadline: This is Gender Regional Curatorial Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:This is Gender\, the photography and visual culture platform ru
 n by Global 50/50\, is launching its inaugural Regional Curatorial Fellows
  Programme for early-career curators and cultural practitioners working ac
 ross photography\, visual culture and gender justice. The premise: images 
 shape how gender\, power and social realities are understood\, feeding pub
 lic debate\, cultural memory\, research\, advocacy and policy\, yet the co
 mmunities and visual practices that should inform those narratives stay un
 derrepresented in the institutions that circulate them. The programme's ar
 gument is that diversifying who makes images is not enough\, and that it a
 lso matters who selects\, contextualises\, writes about\, commissions and 
 circulates them. For the first time\, This is Gender is embedding regional
  curators directly inside its global photography competition\, which has r
 un six cycles and receives thousands of submissions a year. WHAT THE FELLO
 WSHIP INVOLVES: working inside the competition and seeing first-hand how a
  major platform discovers\, reviews and champions new work\; shaping curat
 orial decisions by bringing regional knowledge and cultural insight into t
 he selection and interpretation of work\; contributing to public-facing pr
 ojects including exhibitions\, editorial outputs and digital storytelling 
 while developing your own writing\, framing and curatorial voice\; direct 
 engagement with international judges\, curators\, artists\, editors and cu
 ltural practitioners\; and building a network across an international coho
 rt of peers. WHO SHOULD APPLY: curators\, critics\, writers\, researchers\
 , artistic directors\, photographers\, organisers and cultural practitione
 rs from global majority countries and historically underrepresented commun
 ities. Early-career practitioners and recent graduates are strongly encour
 aged\, and a formal curatorial title is not required. HOW TO APPLY: read t
 he full role description and FAQs on the fellowship page and submit the on
 line application form\; the organisers offer the application in alternativ
 e formats and are open to a conversation before applying. Applications clo
 se 22 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: This is Gender (Global 50/50)\nLoca
 tion: Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competiti
 on. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority countri
 es and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Africa
 \, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities w
 orldwide.\nAward: Stipend of up to GBP 3\,000 for the seven-month part-tim
 e fellowship. No application fee.\nDuration: Seven months from October 202
 6\, part-time and flexible\n\nMore: https://thisisgender.global5050.org/re
 gional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://thisisgender.global5050.org/regional-curatorial-fellowship/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and part-time\, working within a global photography competi
 tion. Applications are invited from practitioners in global majority count
 ries and historically underrepresented communities\, including Asia\, Afri
 ca\, Latin America\, the Middle East\, Oceania\, and diasporic communities
  worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bow-seat-true-blue-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211016Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: True Blue Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:Bow Seat's True Blue Fellowship funds youth-led projects that u
 se the creative arts as their primary method for building awareness or dri
 ving action on ocean\, waterways and climate change issues\, including env
 ironmental justice. Twelve fellows are selected each year and receive up t
 o USD 2\,500 in project funding alongside mentorship\, capacity-building p
 rogramming\, and access to a peer community that includes the Future Blue 
 Youth Council and Bow Seat staff. ELIGIBILITY: youth worldwide aged 16 to 
 24 at the start of the fellowship period in January. Individuals\, groups 
 and youth-led organisations may apply\, with one designated project leader
  for a group. Projects must be new or emerging\, meaning less than two yea
 rs old\, and should span at least one year running January to December. Ap
 plications must be submitted in English\, though translation tools are acc
 eptable\, and applicants should be comfortable with spoken English for pee
 r collaboration. NOTE: this is a youth programme with an upper age limit o
 f 24\, and the creative-arts requirement is central rather than incidental
 \, so proposals built on other methods are out of scope. Deadline 30 Augus
 t 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: Bow Seat Ocean Awareness Programs\n
 Location: Remote\, open to youth worldwide.\nAward: Up to USD 2\,500 in pr
 oject funding per fellow\, plus mentorship and capacity-building programmi
 ng. Twelve youth are selected annually. No application fee.\nDuration: One
  year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https://bowseat.org/programs/tru
 e-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://bowseat.org/programs/true-blue-fellowship/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to youth worldwide.
